gs that we have studied in uh  in in my project for sure greetings are  hugely variable  uh but if you look at a the structural  functional aspects of greetings  you will see that each culture has the  same problems to solve with  greetings showing reciprocity showing  recognition  and identification organizing the entry  in  interaction in a coordinated way so  those are the kind of uh  basic principles uh that uh greetings  in a way make uh make possible  and the way in which the format in which  they make  this this is made possible might be  subject of huge variation  and so if you if you uh use rather in  your culture  haptic formats so formats where you  touch a lot  which means you interact in close  distance  you might have more difficulties than in  a culture where  uh haptic formats are not so important  at the end of the day it's the  connection we're all looking for  how severe is the impact of social  distancing on human connection  yeah in the social sciences we are  pretty critical of the notion of social  distancing  because what we are witnessing with  kovid is a physical distancing  which is not always and not  automatically  social distancing it has of course a  social aspect it has a cultural  uh aspect but we have to think about the  fact that uh  distance is variable in social life  there are activities that are naturally  distant  other uh which need proximity and the  problem  with kovid is that we have these  measures imposing us  at two meters uh of of distance  which are perfectly implementable for  certain activities  much less for others and the decision  how to implement these measures  is a decision that people must take  locally  and this generates interesting but also  difficult  uh normative dilemmas uh that people are  confronted with  at some say so this is all going to be  over i hope  will there be long-term effects will  some of this be  here to stay  well of course it's difficult to say  there are  responses going in two different  directions  one is that kovid has been a revelatory  experience  for us as scientists but also as a  citizen  about what is fundamental in sociality  that we want to keep so we want to come  back  to a normal sociality and in a way those  values  were invisible before and covid has made  us  uh uh conscious about them but there are  also other things that have emerged  during covet kobit has been a natural  laboratory  for experimenting alternative forms of  social life  and some of the most creative will for  sure  stay dorenza mandata thank you very much  thank you to you time to answer more of  your questions now  over to our science correspondent derek  williams  how would the pandemic end only when the  entire world is vaccinated  when it comes to predicting the future  really your guess  is as good as mine but if i did have to  hazard one i'd say  there are a couple of scenarios to  choose from  the first and least likely in my opinion  is that will somehow manage to begin  coordinating efforts on a hitherto  unheard of scale to stymie  new outbreaks before they can spin out  of control  like they have already in country after  country any return to anything like a  pre-pandemic  normal at least on a global scale  will require speeding up and extending  vaccine drives in every corner of the  planet now  and not later and of course the virus  would have to play along by by not  mutating too quickly or or dramatically  so so an imaginable scenario but  one that a lot of epidemiologists think  isn't going to happen  more likely they say is that vaccines  won't stop covet 19 transmission  completely but they will  slow it in many places enough to manage  the virus  like we manage other diseases just think  um hiv aids or or measles  in that scenario soros cov2 would become  endemic and and possibly seasonal like  the flu  and would have to be held in check by  active measures like  like regular vaccine updates and as yet  undeveloped medications people  woul